---
import Callout from '../Callout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../CodeBlock.astro';
import StepCard from '../StepCard.astro';
---

<div class="sec" id="s3">
  <div class="num">03</div>
  <h2>Claude Code basics</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Claude Code is the agent. It runs in a project folder and does the work.
  </p>
  <p>
    Claude Code is a program from Anthropic that runs Claude as an agent on your
    computer. You open it inside a folder, type what you want and it reads,
    writes and runs things in that folder. It is text based, which looks
    intimidating for about ten minutes and then feels like chatting.
  </p>
  <p>
    It needs a paid Claude plan (at the time of writing, Pro or Max) or a pay as
    you go developer account. Plans and prices change, so check <a
      href="https://docs.claude.com">docs.claude.com</a
    > before you buy anything.
  </p>
  <h3>Install and open it</h3>
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="1" title={'Open the terminal'}
      ><p>
        The terminal is a window where you type commands. On a Mac it is called
        Terminal (search for it with Spotlight). On Windows use PowerShell.
      </p></StepCard
    >
    <StepCard number="2" title={'Run the install command from the docs'}
      ><p>
        The Claude Code page on <a href="https://docs.claude.com"
          >docs.claude.com</a
        > has a one line install command for Mac, Windows and Linux. Copy it from
        there rather than from here, because it changes.
      </p></StepCard
    >
    <StepCard number="3" title={'Make a project folder'}
      ><p>
        Create a folder for this project only, for example <code class="i"
          >my-site</code
        > in your Documents. Everything Claude Code does stays inside it.
      </p></StepCard
    >
    <StepCard number="4" title={'Go into that folder'}
      ><p>
        In the terminal type <code class="i">cd</code>, a space, then drag the
        folder into the window and press Enter. That moves the terminal into the
        folder.
      </p></StepCard
    >
    <StepCard number="5" title={'Start Claude Code'}
      ><p>
        Type <code class="i">claude</code> and press Enter. The first time it will
        ask you to sign in with your Claude account.
      </p></StepCard
    >
  </div>
  <CodeBlock label={'Terminal'} code={'cd ~/Documents/my-site\nclaude'} copy />
  <h3>Four habits that make it go well</h3>
  <div class="card" style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px">
    <div>
      <div
        style="font-family: VT323, Courier New, monospace; font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.1; color: var(--color-7dffa8)"
      >
        Ask for a plan before changes
      </div><p style="color: var(--color-9ee6b5)">
        End your request with "show me a plan first and wait for my ok". Claude
        Code will list what it intends to do. Read it. If a step looks wrong,
        say so before anything happens. The docs also describe a plan mode you
        can switch on so it never edits without asking.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div
        style="font-family: VT323, Courier New, monospace; font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.1; color: var(--color-7dffa8)"
      >
        Approve or reject each edit
      </div><p style="color: var(--color-9ee6b5)">
        When it wants to change a file or run a command, it asks. Yes lets it
        through and no stops it. You can type why. You are never obliged to
        accept.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div
        style="font-family: VT323, Courier New, monospace; font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.1; color: var(--color-7dffa8)"
      >
        Give the project standing instructions in CLAUDE.md
      </div><p style="color: var(--color-9ee6b5)">
        CLAUDE.md is a plain text file in your project folder that Claude Code
        reads every time it starts. Put in the things you would otherwise
        repeat: what the project is, what to avoid, how you like things written.
        Typing <code class="i">/init</code> inside Claude Code creates a starter one
        for you.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div
        style="font-family: VT323, Courier New, monospace; font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.1; color: var(--color-7dffa8)"
      >
        Use skills for repeatable jobs
      </div><p style="color: var(--color-9ee6b5)">
        A skill is a reusable pack of instructions you can call by name. I keep
        one that holds my writing style rules, so every document Claude writes
        for me sounds like me instead of like a robot. You could make one for
        "how I like my photos laid out". The docs explain where skill files
        live. One warning: skills written for older models tend to over explain
        and over constrain, which can make a newer model worse. Keep yours short
        and revisit them when the model changes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Example CLAUDE.md'}
    code={"# About this project\n\nThis is NZB's personal website. One page, plain HTML and CSS, no frameworks.\n\n# Rules\n\n- Always show me a plan and wait for my ok before changing files.\n- Keep explanations short and in plain English. I am not a programmer.\n- Do not add anything I did not ask for.\n- Never touch the photos folder."}
    copy
  />
  <Callout title={'If it offers to skip permissions, say no'} warning
    ><svg
      slot="icon"
      width="22"
      height="22"
      viewBox="0 0 24 24"
      fill="none"
      stroke="var(--color-ffb347)"
      stroke-width="2"
      stroke-linecap="round"
      stroke-linejoin="round"
      ><path d="M12 9v4"></path><path d="M12 17h.01"></path><path
        d="M10.3 3.9 1.8 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.7 3h17a2 2 0 0 0 1.7-3L13.7 3.9a2 2 0 0 0-3.4 0z"
      ></path></svg
    ><p>
      There are settings that let Claude Code run without asking. Leave them
      off. The asking is the safety net.
    </p></Callout
  >
</div>
